1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,096
Why should one person never switch a merchant on alone?

2
00:00:03,146 --> 00:00:08,304
Some company cheques need two signatures, so one person alone cannot spend the money.

3
00:00:08,354 --> 00:00:11,856
Switching a merchant on works the same way: four eyes.

4
00:00:11,906 --> 00:00:15,528
One bank admin, the maker, opens a request.

5
00:00:15,578 --> 00:00:17,400
Nothing changes yet.

6
00:00:17,450 --> 00:00:22,872
A different admin, the checker, approves it, and only then is the merchant active.

7
00:00:22,922 --> 00:00:29,064
The maker cannot approve their own request: the screen does not offer it, and the server refuses it anyway.

8
00:00:29,114 --> 00:00:32,568
A request that nobody decides in time expires.

9
00:00:32,618 --> 00:00:37,560
On approval, the documents are checked again, in case something changed meanwhile.

10
00:00:37,610 --> 00:00:42,144
And the history names both people, the one who asked and the one who approved.

11
00:00:42,194 --> 00:00:44,928
Next: how a merchant's server logs in.
